text:
maria: FYI customer reported tool-calling failures when using OpenAI-compatible model. They get a schema validation error in our compat layer, example attached. Seems like the model sometimes returns a different shape (:eyes:)

jon: Can you paste the raw model response and the API request? quick repro helps.

maria: Pasting minimal repro (redacted key). Note: customer set 'tools' list and used model 'gpt-compat-3.5'.

```bash
curl -s -X POST https://api.redwood.example/v1/chat/completions \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $REDWOOD_KEY" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "model": "gpt-compat-3.5",
    "messages": [{"role":"user","content":"lookup book by isbn"}],
    "tools": [{"name":"isbn_lookup","schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"isbn":{"type":"string"}}}}]
  }'
```

maria: Customer gets 400 from our toolcall normalizer. Their raw model response (trimmed):

```json
{
  "id":"c1",
  "choices":[
    {
      "message":{
        "role":"assistant",
        "content":null,
        "tool_calls":[
          {
            "name":"isbn_lookup",
            "arguments":"{\"isbn\":\"9780143127741\"}"
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

maria: Note two oddities vs our OpenAI-compatible expectations:
- we expect a single object at choices[0].message.tool_call with keys name + arguments (object), not an array 'tool_calls'
- arguments is a string (stringified JSON) not an object, so our JSON schema validate fails

eli: We've seen both variations: older/other compat endpoints sometimes return tool_calls (array) or top-level tool_call (object). Also arguments sometimes come as already-parsed object, sometimes string. Our normalizer currently only handles choices[0].message.tool_call.arguments as object.

jon: Repro locally, confirmed. Our validator rejects because schema has "additionalProperties": false and expects arguments to match tool schema precisely.

eli: Short-term workaround for customers: try post-processing on the client to accept stringified args (JSON.parse) and take first element if tool_calls is an array. But that's not great for users.

jon: Proposed fix for compat layer:
- accept both choices[0].message.tool_call (object) and choices[0].message.tool_calls (array) — if array, use first element
- if arguments is a string -> try JSON.parse, fallback to keep string
- normalize enum/tool name casing (lowercase) before lookup
- log a debug metric for 'toolcall_shape' with tags (tool_calls|tool_call|string_args)

maria: Example of normalization sketch:

```js
function normalizeToolCall(message) {
  const tc = message.tool_call || (message.tool_calls && message.tool_calls[0]);
  if (!tc) return null;
  let args = tc.arguments;
  if (typeof args === 'string') {
    try { args = JSON.parse(args); } catch (e) { /* leave as-is */ }
  }
  return { name: tc.name && tc.name.toLowerCase(), arguments: args };
}
```

eli: Also note some models return tool call data nested under "tool_call" inside choices[0].message.content as a markdown block — rare but seen. We should defensively scan message.content for a JSON tool_call block as last resort.

jon: I'll open an eng-runtime ticket to add the compat heuristics + metrics. Can someone add a small support doc so we can forward customers a mitigation? Also we should make the normalizer tolerant but not permissive: if parsed arguments don't match tool schema, surface a clear error with raw payload attached.

maria: I'll draft the support reply (short mitigation + ask for raw response if still failing). Patch request please.

ops-bot: deploy-notify: patch branch 'toolcall/compat-normalizer' created by jon; CI queued.

jon: Assigning to eng-runtime, PR soon. I'll include unit tests for these shapes and a regression test using the customer's raw payload.

maria: Thanks team :thumbsup: I'll follow up with customer once PR merges.
